; Benchmark suites. One thumper executable per subject; committed .thumper
; baselines are the contract, reviewed like goldens. Tier A is ALLOC-gated:
; every suite budgets allocation (no_more_alloc_than 0, tightened to exact for
; the deterministic suites; a small tolerance for bench_session_list, whose scan
; carries variable-length filesystem paths) and gives wall and cpu a budget so
; wide it never fires (no_slower_than 1000). Thumper still records wall and cpu,
; but they never gate, because allocation is exactly reproducible and
; machine-independent while wall time on a shared runner is not — an IO-heavy
; case under load can run several times slower, which is noise, not a regression;
; gating on it is theater.
; The gate runs --quick (11 timed batches instead of 20): allocation is exact
; after a handful of samples, so a fast preset measures it reliably, and
; allocation is the real gate here — the timing budgets are report-only. An
; inconclusive time verdict never fails the rule; an allocation regression
; fails it directly; an improvement writes <suite>.thumper.corrected that
; (diff?) exposes for `dune promote`.
; Baselines are generated with the same --quick preset the gate uses, so a
; clean re-run is Equivalent with no spurious promote candidate.

(executable
 (name bench_path)
 (modules bench_path)
 (libraries thumper lpath))

(executable
 (name bench_digest)
 (modules bench_digest)
 (libraries thumper mentat_digest bench_support))

(executable
 (name bench_diff)
 (modules bench_diff)
 (libraries thumper textdiff bench_support))

(executable
 (name bench_resume)
 (modules bench_resume)
 (libraries thumper mentat_session jsont jsont.bytesrw bench_support))

